<section
  id='case-petart'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Pet Art With Heart — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Real-world artist website case study focused on responsive front-end development, artwork-led presentation, performance, accessibility, and search visibility.'
  data-case='petart'
  aria-labelledby='case-petart-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-petart-title'>Pet Art With Heart</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A responsive business website for discovering and commissioning hand-painted pet
          portraits.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand, UX/UI, content, and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent real-world business site</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>Ongoing</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>HTML, CSS, responsive images</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Live commission portfolio</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='petart'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Pet Art With Heart sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='petart'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Pet Art With Heart needed to work as both an art portfolio and a practical business website.
        Visitors should be able to understand the style of the portraits, learn what a commission
        involves, and find a clear way to make contact. The artwork had to lead the experience, but
        not at the expense of useful information or straightforward navigation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Detailed paintings need large, high-quality images to be appreciated properly. Those
            same images can make a page slow, especially on mobile. I had to balance visual quality
            with loading performance while building enough trust around a personal service. The site
            could not rely on the artwork alone; visitors also needed to know who was behind it,
            what they could order, and how to continue.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Give the portraits visual priority without hiding essential service information</li>
            <li>Deliver detailed artwork efficiently across different screen sizes</li>
            <li>Create a direct path from discovering the work to asking about a commission</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--desktop'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-petart-600.webp'
            data-src='site-petart-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart desktop homepage presenting commissioned pet portraits and introductory content'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Artwork-led desktop homepage</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I used semantic HTML and a clear heading structure so the content remains easy to
              understand and navigate without depending on JavaScript. This also gives search
              engines useful context about the artwork and commission service.
            </li>
            <li>
              The custom responsive layout gives the portraits enough space while keeping the
              supporting text readable. Typography, spacing, and image placement were adjusted
              deliberately rather than fitted into a general-purpose template.
            </li>
            <li>
              Responsive image files reduce unnecessary downloads on smaller screens. Lazy loading
              and asynchronous decoding keep artwork further down the page from delaying the initial
              view.
            </li>
            <li>
              I kept decorative movement lightweight and CSS-based. It adds some warmth to the
              brand, but the portraits remain the strongest visual element and the interface stays
              usable when animation is unavailable.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--mobile'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-petartmobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-petartmobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='515'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart mobile homepage and navigation presenting pet portrait artwork'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Mobile homepage and navigation</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished site brings the artwork, brand, service information, and contact journey
        together in one responsive experience. Because this is my own working business rather than a
        fictional brief, every design decision has a practical consequence. Images need to show
        enough detail to represent the paintings honestly, while the surrounding content has to
        answer real questions and establish trust. The project has been a useful reminder that an
        expressive website still needs restraint: the design should give the work a setting, not
        compete with it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>